  "slug": "gene-rna-d705",
  "summary": "Recent reports link piRNA, H3K9me3, H2A.Z and RNA-binding protein pathways to tissue-specific control of gene expression and transposon silencing.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how chromatin and RNA-based mechanisms establish tissue-specific gene expression programs in germline and somatic tissues. In mouse germ cells, COPS5 loss downregulates piRNA pathway genes, reduces pachytene piRNAs, activates LINE1 and IAP transposons, and arrests spermatogenesis. In Drosophila female germ cells, IDC, STWL and PZG recruit SETDB1 via WDE to deposit H3K9me3 silencing at phf7, a locus-specific domain controlling female identity. Maize H2A.Z mapping links the variant to tissue-specific expression, enhancers and photosynthesis genes in leaf and endosperm. The Drosophila Marf1 study addresses an RNA-binding protein newly expressed in the male accessory gland and its integration into regulatory networks. Together these reports show specific factors directly governing silencing outcomes in defined cellular contexts.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
